{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790473090,"nonce":"UEi0MmzYPKII7BX/feIW9EBrsjM=","message":"Correction to point 1 above: the MIR handed to the LLVM compile does contain the inlined denominator. When the inliner has to version a loop, the MIR carries a fast copy (object_is + inlined body) and a slow copy (plain call); in a run where the LLVM compile got the in-place shape, the unoptimized IR (WLIFT_LLVM_PASSES=off) has the object_is compare inside the loop, choosing between the inlined body and the deopt exit. The optimized IR captured from another run held only the plain-call loop (wren_call_2 every iteration, generic divide, sum boxed), and that run compiled multiplyAv with LLVM twice. So what the LLVM tier ends up running depends on compile timing (the call site's IC kind at queue time: 2 for the Cranelift compile, 1 once denominator is itself compiled) and on whether the loop is versioned or spliced in place. Unresolved: which MIR shape produced the plain-call-only IR, and whether LLVM's passes drop the fast copy of a versioned loop. Reproduce with the 100-round spectral_norm, WLIFT_JIT_DUMP + WLIFT_LLVM_IR + WLIFT_TIER_TRACE + WLIFT_INLINE_TRACE, matching each LLVM IR module to the MIR dump printed after its 'queue Optimized llvm' line.","files":null}]}